From ebd91f8d56ebb043ff092826fba03ab973702f67 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hermes Agent <hermes-agent@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 13:25:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix(gateway): apply slash-access policy to plugin
 commands
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The cold-path slash-access gate resolves access on the name the user
typed (`is_gateway_known_command(canonical)`), and that covers plugin
commands — but only when the typed name matches the registered one.
Plugin dispatch normalizes underscores to hyphens before looking the
handler up, because Telegram's command menu surfaces a hyphenated plugin
command as `/my_cmd` and sends that underscored form back. So for every
hyphenated plugin command the typed name (`my_cmd`) resolves to nothing
while the dispatched name (`my-cmd`) resolves to a live handler, and the
dispatch sink runs it with no policy check at all.

Reproducible symptom: with `allow_admin_from` configured and
`user_allowed_commands` empty, a non-admin typing `/my-cmd` is denied
while the same user typing `/my_cmd` runs the plugin command — on
Telegram, the platform whose menu surfaces plugin commands, the
underscored form is the one users actually get.

Re-check `_check_slash_access` on the normalized name at the sink, right
before dispatch, mirroring the quick-command gate a few lines above:
gate off unless the operator configured it, admins unrestricted,
`user_allowed_commands` honoured on the canonical (hyphenated) name,
everyone else denied before the handler runs. An exactly-typed plugin
command is now checked twice with the same verdict — the same harmless
overlap quick commands already have.

Gating on the normalized name only works if the allowlist is normalized
the same way, so fold `_` to `-` on both sides of the comparison in
`gateway/slash_access.py`: `_coerce_command_list` stores the folded name
alongside its existing slash-stripping and lowercasing, and `can_run`
folds the name it is asked about. Without this, an operator who wrote
`user_allowed_commands: [my_cmd]` — the spelling Telegram's menu showed
them — would get a hard denial for a command they had explicitly
allowed, because the gate compares against `my-cmd`. The folding is not
plugin-specific: several built-ins already register both spellings as
aliases of one command (`reload-mcp`/`reload_mcp`,
`codex-runtime`/`codex_runtime`) while the gate only ever sees the
canonical name, so listing the alias silently granted nothing. Only
names differing solely in those two separators are treated as one; the
allowlist still grants exactly the commands it names.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
